/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:4:The fundamental theorem of algebra states that the number of complex roots of a univariate polynomial is the degree of the polynomial, counting multiplicities.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:6:Enumerative algebraic geometry studies the further generalization of counting solutions to polynomial systems with geometric meaning.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:7:The most elegant results in enumerative algebraic geometry, such as the fundamental theorem of algebra and B\'ezout's Theorem, depend on working over an algebraically closed field, so they are of limited use in applications which require information about real solutions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:13:The inelegance of counting the real roots of a polynomial compared to counting complex roots is typical of statements in enumerative real algebraic geometry.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:15:As a result, the enumerative theory of real algebraic geometry is not as well formed as its complex companion.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:20:Eremenko and Gabrielov proved partial results \cite{EG2002b}, and the full conjecture for the real Schubert calculus of Grassmannians was proved by Mukhin, Tarasov, and Varchenko \cite{MTV2009a,MTV2009b}.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:21:The Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem states that a Schubert problem has all solutions real and distinct if the Schubert varieties involved are defined with respect to distinct real flags osculating a single real parametrized rational normal curve.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:22:Thus the number of real solutions to the corresponding system of real polynomials depends only on the Schubert problem, and this number may be obtained using the Littlewood-Richardson rule.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:24:Computational projects \cite{secant,monotone,RSSS2006} have suggested generalizations to the Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem, some of which now have been proven \cite{EG2002b,mod4,MTV2009a,MTV2009b}.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:25:In this thesis, we describe a computational project extending the study by Eremenko and Gabrielov \cite{EG2002a} of lower bounds on the number of real solutions to certain Schubert problems.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:26:Eremenko and Gabrielov computed a topological degree which gives a lower bound for the number of real points in an intersection of osculating Schubert varieties when the intersection is stable under complex conjugation and at most two of the Schubert varieties are not hypersurfaces.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:27:We solved over 339 million instances of 756 Schubert problems, including those involving non-hypersurface Schubert varieties, to investigate these Eremenko-Gabrielov type lower bounds.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:29:For Schubert problems involving at most two non-hypersurface Schubert varieties, we tested the sharpness of known bounds.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:31:During our computational investigation, we observed that the number of real solutions to a real Schubert problem with certain symmetries is congruent modulo four to the number of complex solutions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:32:This is stronger than the usual congruence modulo two arising from nonreal solutions coming in complex conjugate pairs.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:33:While this congruence was unexpected, the underlying reason is simple enough: there are two involutions acting on the solutions to a real symmetric Schubert problem, complex conjugation and a Lagrangian involution.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:37:Computational complexity can be a serious obstacle when studying systems of equations, and even more so when we investigate systems by the hundreds of millions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:38:As with previous large-scale computations, we were limited by the severe complexity of symbolic computation \cite{HL2011,MM1982}.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:39:Numerical homotopy methods provide an alternative for solving problems which are infeasible by Gr\"obner basis methods in characteristic zero, but the approximate solutions produced do not come with a certificate verifying the solutions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:40:There is software which may be used to certify approximate solutions \cite{alphaCertified}, but the algorithms used require a square polynomial system.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:41:That is, the number of equations must equal the number of variables, and there must be finitely many solutions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:42:Like many other problems in algebraic geometry, Schubert problems are traditionally not defined by a square system.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:44:We give a primal-dual formulation of a Schubert problem which presents it as a square system in local coordinates.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:45:This reformulation is presented in our second main result, Theorem \ref{thm:SchubProbCompInt}, and it allows one to certify approximate solutions obtained numerically.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Introduction.tex:50:In Chapter ~\ref{chapSquare}, we give a method for formulating a general Schubert problem as a square system.
