/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:1:\chapter{SUMMARY} 
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:7:The Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem is a surprisingly elegant result in enumerative real algebraic geometry, which demonstrates that the enumerative theory of real Schubert calculus is a rich field of study.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:10:In recent years, supercomputers have been used to solve billions of polynomial systems in order to investigate problems related to the Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:19:We solved over 339 million instances of 756 Schubert problems, using over 469 gigahertz-years of processing power.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:20:While studying the data, we observed a remarkable congruence modulo four in the number of real solutions to problems with certain symmetries, and this congruence was the topic of Chapter \ref{chapMod4}.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:21:We also discovered a family of Schubert problems, which has unusual gaps in the numbers of real solutions to real osculating instances.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:24:In Chapter \ref{chapMod4}, we proved a congruence modulo four in the number of real solutions to real osculating instances of Schubert problems given by symmetric Schubert conditions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:26:One would typically expect the number of real solutions to a real osculating instance of a Schubert problem to be fixed modulo two, because nonreal solutions come in pairs.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:27:We discovered that there is a Lagrangian involution which also acts on symmetric Schubert problems.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:28:For a rich family of such problems, the Lagrangian involution and complex conjugation are independent and the nonreal solutions come in sets of four.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:29:Establishing a congruence modulo four on the number of real solutions to real osculating instances, we established a new invariant in enumerative real algebraic geometry.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:32:The computational complexity of calculating a Gr\"obner basis in characteristic zero was a bottleneck, which we hope to overcome through the use of certifiable numerical methods.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:33:Algorithms from Smale's $\alpha$-theory may be used to certify numerical output, when the problem involved is given by a square system of polynomial equations.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:34:However, Schubert problems are famously overdetermined.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:36:In Chapter \ref{chapSquare}, we recast instances of Schubert problems as solution sets to square systems.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:37:While this has the practical application of allowing us to use numerical methods in a pure mathematical study of Schubert calculus, our ability to reformulate such a problem as a square system is interesting by its own right.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:38:The duality between $V$ and $V^*$ induces a duality between Schubert varieties in a Grassmannian and a dual Grassmannian, and we use this to give a primal-dual formulation for an instance of a Schubert problem.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:40:The square system of equations may be used to certify approximate solutions obtained via an overdetermined system of determinantal equations, but if the bilinear equations provide a more efficient setting for solving instances via numerical methods, then we may do away with the overdetermined system altogether.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:3:The Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem (previously the Shapiro Conjecture) asserts that a Schubert problem has all solutions distinct and real if the Schubert varieties involved osculate a rational normal curve at real points.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:5:Our purpose is to uncover generalizations of the Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem, proving them when possible.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:6:We also improve the state of the art of computationally solving Schubert problems, allowing us to more effectively study ill-understood phenomena in Schubert calculus.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:8:We use supercomputers to methodically solve real osculating instances of Schubert problems.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:9:By studying over 300 million instances of over 700 Schubert problems, we amass data significant enough to reveal possible generalizations of the Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem and compelling enough to support our conjectures.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:11:To improve the efficiency of solving Schubert problems, we reformulate an instance of a Schubert problem as the solution set to a square system of equations in a higher-dimensional space.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:13:During our investigation, we found the number of real solutions to an instance of a symmetrically defined Schubert problem is congruent modulo four to the number of complex solutions.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:15:We also discovered a family of Schubert problems whose number of real solutions to a real osculating instance has a lower bound depending only on the number of defining flags with real osculation points.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:18:Furthermore, we point out that our square formulation for instances of Schubert problems may facilitate future experimentation by allowing one to solve instances using certifiable numerical methods in lieu of more computationally complex symbolic methods.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/Abstract.tex:19:Additionally, the methods we use for proving the congruence modulo four and for producing an unexpected square system of equations are both quite general, and they may be of use in future projects.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:1:The Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem \ref{Th:MTV} states that an instance of a Schubert problem on a Grassmannian involving Schubert varieties osculating a rational normal curve $\gamma$ at real points has all solutions real.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:2:Intersections involving complex osculation points with the nonreal points coming in pairs may be real varieties, but they typically contain some nonreal points.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:3:While the number of real points in such intersections may not be an invariant of the Schubert problem, there may be related invariants such as the topological lower bound on the number of real solutions in Theorem \ref{Th:EG} given by Eremenko and Gabrielov.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:4:We study real instances of Schubert problems with the goal of understanding these invariants.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:5:Thus we describe real osculating instances of Schubert problems as real enumerative problems and give a method for solving them.
/tmp/paper1307.3xe5rF/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:7:\section{Real Osculating Instances}
