The stronger congruence modulo four in the number of real solutions is due to a geometric involution which we explain in Chapter \ref{chapMod4}.
Thus we will prove that $\Sigma(\It\,,\It)$ is not a sharp lower bound for the number of real solutions to a real osculating instance of $(\It^9)$.

The sixth and final topological lower bound which we did not find to be sharp is $\Sigma(\IIIiiiIIt,\It) = 0$ for $(\IIIiiiIIt\,,\It^8)$ having $90$ complex solutions in $\Gr(4,\C^8)$.
