diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3a5990d341b4b88f678cf48123f38fb9321e1459
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+# Exact factor certificates for arXiv:1307.1833
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | rational exact |
+| Computational core | polynomial identity |
+| Certificate | sparse integer polynomial |
+| Intended intuition | decomposition |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section III.4, Theorem III.4.2 |
+
+This generator covers the factorization model in Nickolas Hein's
+[Reality and Computation in Schubert Calculus](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.1833).
+The solver receives the exact rational polynomial whose roots specify a special
+real osculating Schubert instance and must return one sparse monic factor of its
+derivative.  Exact differentiation and polynomial division check the answer.
+The paper explicitly licenses this representation: Theorem III.4.2 identifies
+the real points of the special instance with monic real factorizations of the
+derivative.  The geometric data remain visible, but the search is performed in
+the theorem's algebraic surrogate; this is not claimed as native geometry
+coverage.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Section II.3 supplies the incidence definition, Section II.6 defines osculating
+flags, and Section III.4 fixes the special condition
+`omega=(2,3,...,k,N)`.  Theorem III.4.2 is both the construction license and the
+easy-regime warning: it reduces these solutions to univariate factorization.
+Exact factorization over `Q[t]` is polynomial-time using LLL, so this is not a
+Track A family.  The executable reference uses classical Berlekamp
+factorization over `F_3` and degree-balanced lifting.  At the provisional
+shipping preset it solved 8/8 instances, with median cost 1,143,973 counted
+field operations and 0.450 seconds.
+
+The generator samples sign polynomials `q(y)` and `r(y)`, sets
+`Q(t)=q(t^m)` and `R(t)=t*r(t^m)`, and composes
+`f'(t)=(Q-R)(Q+R)=Q^2-R^2` before integrating exactly.  Someone who notices
+the two exponent residue classes can take two degree-10 polynomial square
+roots in about 247 exact operations.  Without that decomposition, the measured
+generic route performs over a million operations.  Raising `m` enlarges the
+ambient polynomial degree while keeping the 21-term answer fixed.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=3`)
+
+The smallest preset is hand-solvable: differentiate, separate exponents modulo
+3, recognize two squares, and use a difference of squares.  The complete
+rendered instance is:
+
+```text
+Exact factor certificate for a real osculating Schubert instance
+
+Let Q[t] be the ring of one-variable polynomials with rational coefficients.
+For a sparse term list [[c_1,e_1],...,[c_s,e_s]], the pair [c,e] means c*t^e;
+unlisted coefficients are zero. Rational coefficients are written num/den.
+
+The special Schubert instance represented here lives in Gr(k,R^N), with
+N=14 and k=7. Its Schubert conditions are omega=(2,3,...,k,N) at infinity
+and one hypersurface condition at each of the 13 distinct complex roots of
+the following real squarefree osculation-divisor polynomial f(t):
+
+f terms (coefficient, exponent), descending by exponent:
+[[1/13, 13], [-1/5, 10], [-1/9, 9], [-1/7, 7], [-1/3, 6],
+ [1/2, 4], [-1/3, 3], [1, 1], [9789, 0]]
+
+Return a monic degree-6 factor of f'(t) as exactly 5 JSON pairs
+[coefficient, exponent], in strictly descending exponent order. Coefficients
+must be nonzero integers in [-1,1], the first pair must be [1,6], the last
+exponent must be 0, and exponents may not repeat.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as the JSON term list.
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The planted answer is
+`[[1,6],[-1,4],[-1,3],[-1,1],[-1,0]]`.
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Dropping its last term returns
+`(False, "expected exactly 5 nonzero terms")`.  Exact enumeration finds two
+valid bounded-language factors among 160 demo candidates.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+Here `n` is the requested factor degree; the represented Grassmannian is
+`Gr(n+1, R^(2n+2))`.
+
+| preset | n | residue modulus m | compressed degree | answer terms | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 3 | 2 | 5 | hand example |
+| easy | 70 | 7 | 10 | 21 | provisional shipping preset |
+| medium | 110 | 11 | 10 | 21 | locally verified |
+| hard | 130 | 13 | 10 | 21 | locally verified |
+
+The OpenRouter quota failed before the first oracle attempt could be scored,
+so STEP 4 has not confirmed or moved the provisional `easy` shipping preset.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged answer recovered from prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware random candidates |
+| G5 | pass | shipping space 48,499,517,191,478,612,852,736; demo exact count 2; reference 8/8 |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference and compact routes each 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | degree 140 builds and verifies with the same 21 answer terms |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 152 characters, 42 atoms, about 38 tokens, 246 operations on the measured instance |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The script made four retries per arm, but every call was an API error and hence
+correctly consumed **zero** scored attempts.  Error records are preserved; they
+are not evidence that a model failed the problem.
+
+| arm | preset | recorded seeds | solved / scored attempts | why unavailable |
+|---|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 1755348843, 1897738289, 849482379, 454268438 | 0/0 | HTTP 403, OpenRouter key total limit exceeded |
+| structural hint | easy | see `g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl` | 0/0 | same HTTP 403 |
+| placebo hint | easy | see `g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl` | 0/0 | same HTTP 403 |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is therefore undefined, and no conclusion about the hint's
+effect is possible.  The structural hint only names the residue-square
+invariant; it does not give a sequence of steps.  A fresh successful harness
+run is required before this family can be submitted as hardened.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_1307_1833 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=3, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+assert verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful oracle rerun:
+
+```bash
+python3 results/1307.1833/gen_1307_1833.py
+python3 scripts/harden.py results/1307.1833/gen_1307_1833.py
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1307.1833 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is deliberately structured Track B data, not evidence that generic
+Schubert problems or even this special family are complexity-theoretically
+hard.  The factorization theorem, an LLL implementation, a CAS, or recognition
+of the two residue classes makes it easy.  The 0/200,000 estimate applies only
+to the declared prior: uniformly sampled monic sparse sign polynomials with
+the exact term count and a nonzero constant.  It is not a bound for informed
+guesses, and the structural distribution is much narrower than that language.
+
+The measured reference is modular Berlekamp plus lifting, not a Gröbner basis,
+homotopy continuation, or a production LLL factorizer; those heavier Schubert
+and algebraic attacks were not run.  The adversary panel covers coefficient
+outliers, a one-square greedy guess, 256 structured restarts, and a
+palindromic/alternating ansatz.  Canonicalization proves invariance under input
+term reordering and `t -> -t`, but not the full projective linear action because
+general translations and scalings do not preserve the bounded sparse witness
+language.  Finally, the oracle quota failure means hardness against the
+required four-vendor no-tool pool remains unverified.
