/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-3-  \label{chapter: Summary}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-4-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-5-In Chapter \ref{chapSchubert}, we gave background needed to understand our study in enumerative real algebraic geometry.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-6-We outlined the history surrounding some theorems and conjectures in Schubert calculus.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-7-The Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem is a surprisingly elegant result in enumerative real algebraic geometry, which demonstrates that the enumerative theory of real Schubert calculus is a rich field of study.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-8-This remarkable theorem was not generally accepted when it was first conjectured, and computations played a large role in giving credence to it.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-9-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-10-In recent years, supercomputers have been used to solve billions of polynomial systems in order to investigate problems related to the Mukhin-Tarasov-Varchenko Theorem.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-11-These investigations have lead to theorems and strongly supported conjectures.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-12-We continued this practice of studying reality problems with the use of supercomputers.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-13-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-14-In Chapter \ref{chapLower}, we described a study of Eremenko and Gabrielov, which used topological methods to obtain lower bounds to the number of real points in a fiber of the Wronski map over a real point.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-15-We realized this inverse Wronski problem as a problem in Schubert calculus and used modern software tools to investigate these bounds from a more general point of view.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-16-We discovered that the Eremenko-Gabrielov type lower bounds are often sharp.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-17-In some cases, however, sharpness fails in an interesting way.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-18-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-19-We solved over 339 million instances of 756 Schubert problems, using over 469 gigahertz-years of processing power.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-20-While studying the data, we observed a remarkable congruence modulo four in the number of real solutions to problems with certain symmetries, and this congruence was the topic of Chapter \ref{chapMod4}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-21-We also discovered a family of Schubert problems, which has unusual gaps in the numbers of real solutions to real osculating instances.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex:22:These relate to work of Sottile and Soprunova, and we used their method of counting real factorizations of a real polynomial to explain the observed lower bounds and gaps.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-23-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-24-In Chapter \ref{chapMod4}, we proved a congruence modulo four in the number of real solutions to real osculating instances of Schubert problems given by symmetric Schubert conditions.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-25-This work affirmed the most surprising and compelling conjecture to come out of the computational project described in Chapter \ref{chapLower}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-26-One would typically expect the number of real solutions to a real osculating instance of a Schubert problem to be fixed modulo two, because nonreal solutions come in pairs.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-27-We discovered that there is a Lagrangian involution which also acts on symmetric Schubert problems.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-28-For a rich family of such problems, the Lagrangian involution and complex conjugation are independent and the nonreal solutions come in sets of four.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-29-Establishing a congruence modulo four on the number of real solutions to real osculating instances, we established a new invariant in enumerative real algebraic geometry.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-30-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-31-The work in Chapter \ref{chapLower}, and a lot of other work done in Schubert calculus, relied heavily on formulating problems in a way that is efficient for computation.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-32-The computational complexity of calculating a Gr\"obner basis in characteristic zero was a bottleneck, which we hope to overcome through the use of certifiable numerical methods.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-33-Algorithms from Smale's $\alpha$-theory may be used to certify numerical output, when the problem involved is given by a square system of polynomial equations.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-34-However, Schubert problems are famously overdetermined.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-35-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-36-In Chapter \ref{chapSquare}, we recast instances of Schubert problems as solution sets to square systems.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-37-While this has the practical application of allowing us to use numerical methods in a pure mathematical study of Schubert calculus, our ability to reformulate such a problem as a square system is interesting by its own right.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-38-The duality between $V$ and $V^*$ induces a duality between Schubert varieties in a Grassmannian and a dual Grassmannian, and we use this to give a primal-dual formulation for an instance of a Schubert problem.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-39-This requires that we work in a larger space, adding variables, but we benefit from replacing higher-degree determinantal equations by bilinear equations.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/Summary.tex-40-The square system of equations may be used to certify approximate solutions obtained via an overdetermined system of determinantal equations, but if the bilinear equations provide a more efficient setting for solving instances via numerical methods, then we may do away with the overdetermined system altogether.
