/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-555-\section{Gaps}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-556-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-557-Several of the Schubert problems we studied had unexpected gaps in the possible numbers of real solutions.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-558-One may see this in Tables \ref{tab:gap12}--\ref{tab:44.1e7}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-559-That we never have $12$ or $16$ solutions in Table \ref{tab:gap12} is particularly unexpected, as this problem satisfies a congruence modulo four, and $12\equiv 16\equiv 20 \mod 4$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:560:The gaps in all three of these tables may be fully explained by Theorem \ref{thm:factorLB}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-561-Proposition \ref{prop:mod4tech} from Chapter \ref{chapMod4} gives an alternative explanation for the congruence modulo four observed in Tables \ref{tab:gap12} and \ref{tab:22.1e5} (but not for the congruence in Table \ref{tab:44.1e7}).
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-562-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:563:A solution to an instance of the Schubert problem given in Table \ref{tab:gap12} is real if and only if its coordinates are given by a real factorization $f'(t)=A(t)B(t)$ as in Theorem \ref{thm:factorLB}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-564-Since the number $r$ of real factors of $f'(t)$ is at least $r_{\Is}-1$, we have $r=r_{\Is}-1,r_{\Is}+1,\dotsc,6$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:565:For each of these $r$-values, the number of real solutions to $X$ is exactly the lower bound of Corollary \ref{cor:factorLB} associated to the osculation type $(1,r)$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:566:Thus the set of lower bounds given by Corollary \ref{cor:factorLB} is the set of possible numbers of real points in $X$, given by Theorem \ref{thm:factorLB}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-567-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-568-Similar analysis explains the gaps found in Tables \ref{tab:22.1e5} and \ref{tab:44.1e7}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-569-We give an example in a Grassmannian of higher dimension.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-570-\begin{example}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-571- Consider the Schubert condition $\omat$ for $\Gr(5,10)$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:572: The lower bounds of Corollary \ref{cor:factorLB} corresponding to $\balpha = (\omat,\It^{n-1})$ are $6,6,14,30$, or $70$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-573- Thus any real osculating instance of $\balpha$ has exactly $6,14,30$, or $70$ real solutions.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:574: The lower bound of $6$ which is independent of osculation type is an example of the nontrivial lower bound given by Corollary \ref{cor:factorLB}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-575- This lower bound is the topological lower bound $\Sigma(\omat,\It)$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-576-\end{example}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-577-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-578-Let
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-579-\[ 
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-580-  \begin{picture}(46.5,46.5)
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-581-   \put (0,0){\includegraphics{figures/minusOmega.pdf}}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-582-   \put (11.5,2.1){$\cdots$}
