/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-584-  \end{picture}\ \,\raisebox{24pt}{$ =: \lambda(p,q)$}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-585-\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-586-be the skew-diagram with $p$ boxes in the rightmost column and $q$ boxes in the bottom row.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-587-\begin{proposition}\label{prop:LBareEQ}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-588- Let $p=k-1$ and $q=n-k-1$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-589- If $p\geq 2$ or $q\geq 2$, then
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-590- \[\sum_{T\in\SYT(\lambda(p,q))}\sign(T) = \sum_{T\in\SYT(\lambda(p,q-2))}\sign(T) + \sum_{T\in\SYT(\lambda(p-2,q))}\sign(T)\,.\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-591-\end{proposition}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-592-\begin{proof}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-593- We first observe that both sides of the equation are zero unless $p$ and $q$ are even.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-594- Thus we only need to prove one base case $p=q=2$ to use induction.
--
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-673-  \end{picture}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-674- \]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-675-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-676- Let $\mathcal{T}_1\subset\SYT(\lambda(p,q))$ be the set of standard Young tableaux of the first type ($\mbox{Y}$ appears in the single column, and $\mbox{Z}$ appears in the single row).
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-677- Similarly, let $\mathcal{T}_2,\mathcal{T}_3,\mathcal{T}_4\subset\SYT(\lambda)$ denote the sets of tableaux of the second, third, and fourth types respectively.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-678- Since the tableaux of $\mathcal{T}_2$ are obtained by applying the transposition $\mbox{Y} \leftrightarrow \mbox{Z}$ to $\mathcal{T}_1$, we have
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-679- \[\sum_{T\in\mathcal{T}_1}\sign(T) + \sum_{T\in\mathcal{T}_2}\sign(T) = 0\,.\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-680- Therefore, we need only consider the parity of tableaux of the third and fourth types,
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-681- \[\sum_{T\in \SYT(\lambda(p,q))} \sign(T) = \sum_{T\in \mathcal{T}_3} \sign(T) + \sum_{T\in \mathcal{T}_4} \sign(T)\,.\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-682- Deleting the columns of tableaux in $\mathcal{T}_3$ which contain $\mbox{Y},\mbox{Z}$ gives a bijection
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-683- \[\pi_3:\SYT(\lambda(p,q))\rightarrow\SYT(\lambda(p,q-2))\,,\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-684- which one immediately verifies has the property $\sign(\pi_3(T)) = \sign(T)$ for $T\in \SYT(\lambda(p,q))$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-685- Therefore,
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-686- \[\sum_{T\in \SYT(\lambda(p,q))} \sign(T) = \sum_{T\in \SYT(\lambda(p,q-2))} \sign(T) + \sum_{T\in \mathcal{T}_4} \sign(T)\,.\]
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-687- 
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-688- Deleting the rows of $T\in \mathcal{T}_4$ which contain $\mbox{Y},\mbox{Z}$ gives a bijection $\pi_4:\SYT(\lambda(p,q))\rightarrow\SYT(\lambda(p-2,q))$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-689- The standard filling of $\lambda(p,q)$ has the same parity as the tableaux given by assigning $(1,2,\dotsc,p-2,\mbox{Y},\mbox{Z})$ to the column and $(p-1,p,\dotsc,p+q-1)$ to the row.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-690- Thus, $\sign(\pi_4(T)) = \sign(T)$ for $T\in \SYT(\lambda(p,q))$, and we have our result.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-691-\end{proof}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-692-The skew diagram $\lambda(p,q)$ as a product of two chains, one of length $p$ and one of length $q$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex:693:Sottile and Soprunova studied products of chains and showed a connection between lower bounds obtained by factorization and topological lower bounds \cite{SS2006}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-694-The following is a corollary to Proposition \ref{prop:LBareEQ}.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-695-\begin{theorem}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-696- Suppose $k=2p+1$ is odd, $n=2p+2q+2$ is even, and $X$ is the real osculating instance of $\balpha$ from Equation (\ref{eqn:LBfamily}).
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-697- The lower bound $\tbinom{p+q}{p}$ on the number of real points in $X$ coincides with the topological lower bound $\Sigma(\omat,\It)$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-698-\end{theorem}
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-699-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-700-There are Schubert problems $\balpha$ not of the form $(\omat,\It^{n-1})$ whose frequency tables exhibit gaps, sometimes apparently due to unexpected upper bounds on the numbers of real solutions for instances of $\balpha$ with certain osculation types.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-701-Table \ref{tab:upperGap} reporting on the Schubert problem $(\IIIiIt\,,\IIiit\,,\IIit\,,\IIit\,,\It)$ in $\Gr(4,\C^8)$ exhibits such behavior.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-702-
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-703-There are also problems with no gaps which have apparent upper bounds lower than the number of complex solutions for certain osculation types.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-704-Table \ref{tab:upperLower} exhibits remarkable upper bounds for $(\IIit\,,\IIit\,,\It\,,\It\,,\It)$ in $\Gr(3,\C^6)$.
/tmp/arxiv1307.ZgXqKl/src/Chapters/ContentSections/chapLower.tex-705-Unexpected upper bounds were far less common than nontrivial lower bounds in the problems we tested.
