 ## Caveats
 
-- The four local attacks are magnitude outliers, a one-square greedy guess,
-  256 structure-aware random restarts, and a palindromic/alternating by-hand
-  ansatz. A full LLL factorizer, a commercial CAS, and a Gröbner solve of the
-  original flag-incidence system were not run; the first two are expected to
-  succeed and are precisely why this is Track B.
-- The 0/200,000 density estimate is for uniformly sampled sparse sign
-  polynomials with the exact support size, monicity, height, degree, and
-  nonzero constant term visible in the statement. It does not model a solver
-  that has noticed the residue classes; that solver should use the compact
-  route and succeed.
-- The executable Berlekamp reference uses a good prime deliberately ensured by
-  construction and a coefficient-height-one lift. The general polynomial-time
-  fact is supplied by rational LLL factorization, not by a worst-case claim for
-  this particular lift implementation.
-- The solver sees the theorem-licensed polynomial coordinate model, not the
-  full osculating flags. Accordingly the profile says `licensed_reduction`, not
-  `native` domain essentiality.
-- Most importantly, external hardness is unmeasured until OpenRouter quota is
-  restored and all three script-owned runs complete successfully.
+- The 0/200,000 estimate samples uniformly from the exact three-block language,
+  including its fixed lengths, stride, monicity, height bound, and nonzero
+  constant. It does not model a solver that has recognized the fourth-power
+  blocks; that solver should use the compact route and succeed.
+- The local attacks are a coefficient-magnitude projection, greedy residue
+  projection, 256 structured random restarts, and a symmetric/alternating
+  by-hand ansatz. A full LLL factorizer, commercial CAS, and a Groebner solve of
+  the original flag-incidence equations were not run. The first two are expected
+  to succeed and are why this is Track B.
+- The executable reference deliberately uses a good prime certified during
+  generation and a coefficient bound small enough for unique centered lifting.
+  General polynomial-time factorization is supplied by rational LLL theory, not
+  by a worst-case claim for this compact reference implementation.
+- Canonicalization covers input-term reordering and the projective reflection
+  `t -> -t`. General projective changes do not preserve the bounded residue-block
+  answer language and are outside this generator's represented family.
+- Most importantly, external model hardness remains unmeasured until all three
+  OpenRouter runs complete without API errors.
