   182	## Caveats
   183	
   184	- The 0/200,000 estimate samples uniformly from the exact three-block language,
   185	  including its fixed lengths, stride, monicity, height bound, and nonzero
   186	  constant. It does not model a solver that has recognized the fourth-power
   187	  blocks; that solver should use the compact route and succeed.
   188	- The local attacks are a coefficient-magnitude projection, greedy residue
   189	  projection, 256 structured random restarts, and a symmetric/alternating
   190	  by-hand ansatz. A full LLL factorizer, commercial CAS, and a Groebner solve of
   191	  the original flag-incidence equations were not run. The first two are expected
   192	  to succeed and are why this is Track B.
   193	- The executable reference deliberately uses a good prime certified during
   194	  generation and a coefficient bound small enough for unique centered lifting.
   195	  General polynomial-time factorization is supplied by rational LLL theory, not
   196	  by a worst-case claim for this compact reference implementation.
   197	- Canonicalization covers input-term reordering and the projective reflection
   198	  `t -> -t`. General projective changes do not preserve the bounded residue-block
   199	  answer language and are outside this generator's represented family.
   200	- Most importantly, external model hardness remains unmeasured until all three
   201	  OpenRouter runs complete without API errors.

